---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

const idx = projects.findIndex((p) => p.slug === "atlassian");
const project = projects[idx];
const next = projects[(idx + 1) % projects.length];
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL; // "/portfolio-presence/full-time/"

const tldr = [
  { k: "Space", v: "Enterprise ITSM, mobile" },
  {
    k: "Product",
    v: "Mobile on-call scheduling — schedule views, card customisation, overrides — inside a company-level platform consolidation",
  },
  {
    k: "Method",
    v: "Task-based remote moderated usability (90 min) with SEQ ratings, on a prototype, grounded in a synthesis of prior on-call research",
  },
  {
    k: "Sample",
    v: "11 analysed of 12 recruited — 6 on-call engineers, 5 engineering & ops managers, all handling on-call tooling day to day",
  },
  { k: "Partnered with", v: "Product, design, and engineering (triad)" },
  {
    k: "Outcome",
    v: "Reframed mobile on-call priorities; 4 design changes shipped, 2 features added to the roadmap; +15% satisfaction and ~+10% adoption post-launch",
  },
];
---
<Layout
  title="On-call scheduling research — Anmol Bhargava"
  description="Evaluative research on mobile on-call scheduling in enterprise ITSM that shifted the team's question from making scheduling faster to investing in the parts of the on-call workflow mobile actually serves."
>
  <article class="section section--cream">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 820px;">
      <a href={`${base}#work`} class="back-link">← Selected work</a>
      <div class="case-meta">Project {project.num} · {project.meta}</div>
      <h1 class="display display--xl" style="color: var(--brick);">{project.title}</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top: 1rem;">{project.subtitle}</p>
      <div class="case-tags">
        {project.tags.map((t) => <span class="tag">{t}</span>)}
      </div>

      <!-- TL;DR -->
      <div class="tldr">
        <div class="tldr__label">TL;DR</div>
        <div class="tldr__grid">
          {tldr.map((item) => (
            <div>
              <div class="tldr__k">{item.k}</div>
              <div class="tldr__v">{item.v}</div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="tldr__shift">
          <div class="tldr__k">Strategic shift</div>
          <div class="tldr__v">
            “How do we make scheduling faster on mobile?” → “Which parts of the on-call workflow
            does mobile actually carry?”
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- 1. The product problem -->
      <div class="section-label">The product problem</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          An engineering manager pages an on-call engineer at 2am because a critical incident just
          landed. Whether that moment goes well depends on unglamorous things: the engineer knowing
          they're actually on shift, an override that took effect the way both parties expected, a
          schedule that looks the same today as it did yesterday. In enterprise incident
          management, ambiguity isn't a cosmetic flaw — it's operational risk.
        </p>
        <p>
          This study sat inside a company-level bet: consolidating a standalone incident-management
          product into the flagship ITSM platform, with a top-line OKR attached. Mobile was part of
          that bet. The design team had built new on-call scheduling features for the mobile app —
          daily and monthly schedule views, card-view customisation, overrides — reasoning that
          easier, faster access to schedules on the go would strengthen the case for the
          consolidated app.
        </p>
        <p>
          Inside that reasoning lived an assumption worth naming: that scheduling was what mobile
          on-call users needed most, and that making it quicker would translate into adoption.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- 2. The research question -->
      <div class="section-label">The research question</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The tactical question was real and urgent: can on-call engineers and managers find,
          understand, and operate the new scheduling components? Designs were in flight, and the
          team needed that answer at the speed of their decisions.
        </p>
        <p>
          But prior research argued for a second question underneath it. Earlier studies of on-call
          tooling had found that most users opened the mobile app to acknowledge and close alerts —
          only some used it to check who was on call. If that still held, polishing scheduling
          might improve a screen without moving the outcome the team actually cared about.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the study carried two questions at once. The evaluative one: <em>do these components
          work?</em> And the strategic one: <em>where in the on-call workflow does mobile actually
          earn its place — and is scheduling that place?</em>
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- 3. The research strategy -->
      <div class="section-label">The research strategy</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The method was deliberately tactical: 90-minute remote moderated usability sessions,
          task-based, with a Single Ease Question rating after each task. A leaner method matched
          to decision speed, with the rigour invested in how it was run — and in refusing to look
          at the feature in isolation. Every task was read against the workflow around it: what
          participants reached for first, what they ignored, what they said their day actually
          looks like.
        </p>
        <p>
          The sample mirrored the workflow rather than a single persona: on-call engineers, who
          live their own schedule and add overrides, and engineering managers and team leads, who
          plan the team's coverage and change it. In enterprise scheduling these are structurally
          different jobs — one is subject to the schedule, the other is accountable for it — and
          collapsing them into one “user” would have blurred exactly the differences that mattered.
          All participants handled on-call tooling in their day-to-day work, across dedicated
          incident-management platforms, internal tools, and manual schedules.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before fieldwork: stakeholder interviews with the designer and PM to absorb the intent
          behind the flows, and a pilot of both screener and prototype with internal on-call
          engineers. During fieldwork: sessions observed live by the triad and note-takers, and
          tagged in Dovetail as they happened, so synthesis built continuously instead of arriving
          as a cliff at the end. One recruited participant cleared the screener but contradicted it
          in session; their data was excluded, leaving 11 of 12. Small samples only hold up if
          you're strict about what counts.
        </p>
        <p>
          Scope was explicit too. Journey mapping was declared out of scope up front — this study
          tested scheduling designs, and its claims stay inside that boundary.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- 4. What we learned -->
      <div class="section-label">What we learned</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          On the surface, the evaluation passed. Every participant completed the daily-schedule,
          monthly-schedule, and override tasks. Overrides rated easiest (SEQ 5.3 of 7) and drew the
          most unprompted enthusiasm. The one clear usability failure was card-view customisation:
          7 of 11 completed it, 4 never found the settings, and 3 said the feature added no value
          regardless.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the question had stayed “is this usable?”, the study would have ended there. The more
          consequential evidence was in what participants did and said around the tasks.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">Engineers kept drifting toward alerts</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          <span class="mini-label">Observed</span> Asked to check “what their day looks like”,
          four of six engineers went to the alerts tab, and described alerts as the most useful
          part of the app in daily work. One asked whether they could start responding to an alert —
          preliminary investigation — from the phone itself.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="verbatim">
          <p>
            “Having the alerts at the very top and the very home screen — that's probably the
            primary usefulness.”
          </p>
          <div class="verbatim__attr">— P9, on-call engineer</div>
        </blockquote>
        <p class="insight__body">
          <span class="mini-label mini-label--read">The read</span> For engineers, mobile's job in
          on-call work is awareness and response. Schedule administration is occasional; alerts are
          daily. A scheduling-first mobile strategy had that emphasis inverted.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">The workflow runs on predictability, not speed</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          <span class="mini-label">Observed</span> The feature built for speed — taking a shift
          “for an hour” in one tap — got the most ambivalent reception in the study. Participants
          pointed out that schedules are set well in advance; those who liked the option mostly
          asked to customise its duration. Prior research had the same shape: users wanted to see
          their coming weeks so they could plan ahead. Nobody was asking for scheduling to be
          quicker.
        </p>
        <p class="insight__body">
          <span class="mini-label mini-label--read">The read</span> On-call scheduling is a system
          for eliminating surprises. Participants optimised for knowing what's coming, not for
          shaving seconds off actions they take occasionally.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">Learned patterns beat novel ones</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          <span class="mini-label">Observed</span> Participants leaned on visual memory, wanting to
          keep the associations they'd already built between colours and people. Where the
          interface matched expectations — two of the three paths for adding an override — everyone
          succeeded. The third path, which behaved unlike what its control suggested, mostly
          confused the few who tried it.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="verbatim">
          <p>
            “I usually tend to get comfortable with seeing a certain colour, associating that with
            a certain user.”
          </p>
          <div class="verbatim__attr">— P9, on-call engineer</div>
        </blockquote>
        <p class="insight__body">
          <span class="mini-label mini-label--read">The read</span> These teams already juggle six
          or seven tools — one manager listed them from memory. In an ecosystem that taxes
          attention this heavily, consistency and learnability are worth more than cleverness;
          every unfamiliar pattern spends memory that's already spoken for.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">One screen was quietly serving two different jobs</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          <span class="mini-label">Observed</span> Managers were blunt that the app's framing
          didn't fit them: a “my schedules” tab is noise for someone who never takes call. What
          they reached for, repeatedly, was whole-team visibility — everyone's coverage in one
          place, who could fill an empty slot, ideally with project context attached.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="verbatim">
          <p>
            “As a manager, I am never gonna fill in for anybody's absence. I am not gonna sit and
            take incident calls.”
          </p>
          <div class="verbatim__attr">— P7, engineering manager</div>
        </blockquote>
        <p class="insight__body">
          <span class="mini-label mini-label--read">The read</span> Engineers are subject to the
          schedule; managers are accountable for it. One view designed for both actors meant the
          manager half of the workflow had no real home.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- 5. The key insight -->
      <div class="kx">
        <div class="kx__kicker">The key insight</div>
        <div class="kx__eq">
          <span class="kx__term">Predictability + learnability</span>
          <span class="kx__gt">&gt;</span>
          <span class="kx__term kx__term--dim">simply reducing task time</span>
        </div>
        <p class="kx__body">
          What made the mobile experience valuable wasn't completing scheduling tasks faster. It
          was knowing what's coming, and trusting patterns already learned. That's a read across
          converging qualitative signals — task behaviour, verbatims, and prior studies — not a
          measured effect. But every signal pointed the same direction, and none of them pointed
          toward speed.
        </p>
        <ul class="kx__ev">
          <li>Schedules are set well in advance — the one-tap speed feature met shrugs</li>
          <li>Users asked to see weeks ahead so they could plan, in this study and prior ones</li>
          <li>Colour was used as memory: participants wanted learned associations preserved</li>
          <li>Override paths that matched expectations succeeded; the novel one confused</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- 6. How the research changed the product conversation -->
      <div class="section-label">How the research changed the product conversation</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The readout didn't argue the designs were bad — mostly, they worked. It argued the
          question was too narrow. Instead of “how do we make scheduling faster on mobile?”, the
          evidence supported asking “which parts of on-call work does mobile actually carry?” — and
          the answer clustered around overrides, alert awareness, and team-level visibility rather
          than schedule browsing and cosmetic customisation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Concretely, the recommendations: rebuild the manager view around team information rather
          than a personal-schedule frame; fold card detail into tap-to-expand instead of a separate
          settings toggle; add on-demand detail to the monthly view; make the one-tap shift
          takeover duration-customisable. And one deprioritisation: card-view customisation — the
          weakest task in the study — was flagged as low priority, which participants had said
          themselves in nearly as many words.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- 7. Cross-functional collaboration -->
      <div class="section-label">Cross-functional collaboration</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The project ran on a triad — researcher, designer, PM — with the engineering manager
          close. I interviewed the designer and PM before writing the discussion guide, so the
          study tested their actual intent rather than my reading of it. They watched sessions from
          the observer panel, and insights were soft-shared and sparred on before the report was
          final — partly for accuracy, partly because conclusions people help shape travel further
          than conclusions they're handed.
        </p>
        <p>
          The evidence was packaged to be used, not admired: verbatims and Loom clips so users'
          own voices did the persuading, findings framed as problems and how-might-we prompts so
          design could pick them up directly, and a recorded shareback so the insights reached
          people who weren't in the room.
        </p>
        <p>
          The decisions stayed where they belonged — with product and design. Research's job was to
          make sure those decisions were made looking at the workflow users actually have, not the
          one the roadmap assumed.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- 8. Outcome -->
      <div class="section-label">Outcome</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Being precise about what happened next:</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>
          <span>
            <strong>Directly observed:</strong> all core scheduling tasks were operable; overrides
            was the standout (easiest, most valued); card-view customisation failed on findability
            and perceived value.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>
            <strong>Recommended:</strong> a manager view rebuilt around team visibility,
            progressive detail in schedule views, and a duration-customisable shift takeover.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>
            <strong>Deprioritised:</strong> further investment in card-view customisation.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>
            <strong>Shipped by the team:</strong> 4 design changes simplifying navigation and
            interaction patterns, and 2 features added to the product roadmap.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>
            <strong>Post-launch:</strong> +15% user satisfaction and ~+10% feature adoption.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>
            <strong>Ongoing:</strong> research partnered with design to track how the insights
            played into adoption after the consolidation.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <p>
          Two caveats on that ledger, deliberately kept: the satisfaction and adoption figures are
          post-launch measures, not outputs of this study — and the study's claim is to the
          reprioritisation, not the numbers.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- 9. Why this matters -->
      <div class="section-label">Why this matters for enterprise UX research</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Enterprise products are workflows before they are screens. The useful contributions here
          came from treating the study that way: two actors with structurally different jobs, an
          ecosystem of six or seven tools, schedules that encode a team's agreements about risk.
          Feature-level usability was the entry point, not the destination.
        </p>
        <p>
          A feature request is rarely the underlying problem. Participants asked for colour
          customisation; the need underneath was continuity of learned patterns. The team asked
          for faster scheduling; the workflow ran on predictability.
        </p>
        <p>
          The highest-leverage thing research did here wasn't finding usability issues. It was
          helping the team check whether they were solving the right problem while there was still
          time to redirect — and in enterprise B2B, where roadmaps carry OKR-level commitments,
          that check is worth more than polish.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Takeaway -->
      <div class="pullquote">
        <div class="pullquote__label">Takeaway</div>
        <p class="pullquote__text">
          Speed is easy to measure, which makes it easy to over-value. This workflow ran on
          predictability and learned patterns — and research's job was to notice which problem
          actually deserved the screen.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- CTA -->
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a href={`${base}projects/${next.slug}`} class="btn btn--filled-brick">
          Next: {next.title.length > 40 ? next.title.slice(0, 40) + "…" : next.title} →
        </a>
        <a href={`${base}#contact`} class="btn">Get in touch</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .mini-label {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-right: 0.45rem;
  }
  .mini-label--read {
    color: var(--brick);
  }
  .verbatim {
    margin: 1rem 0 1.25rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    background: var(--cream-soft);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--brick);
    border-radius: 0 12px 12px 0;
    max-width: 58ch;
  }
  .verbatim p {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .verbatim__attr {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .kx {
    background: var(--deep);
    color: var(--on-brick);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    padding: 2.5rem 2.25rem;
    margin: 3.5rem 0;
  }
  .kx__kicker {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    opacity: 0.7;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .kx__eq {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.6rem 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .kx__term {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.4vw, 2.2rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }
  .kx__term--dim {
    opacity: 0.55;
  }
  .kx__gt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.4vw, 2.2rem);
    color: var(--peach);
  }
  .kx__body {
    font-size: 0.96rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    opacity: 0.92;
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }
  .kx__ev {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 700px) {
    .kx__ev {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
    }
  }
  .kx__ev li {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    position: relative;
    opacity: 0.85;
  }
  .kx__ev li::before {
    content: "→";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--peach);
    font-weight: 700;
  }
</style>
